.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_SEND_ZC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_send_zc \- send a message to a dynamic queue (no block)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_send_zc(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq, c_message_t *msg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Puts the message at the tail of the dynamic queue and returns at once.
The queue has no size limit, so there is never a wait for free space,
and this is the only send call of the dynamic queue: there is no
blocking send and no send with a timeout.

On success the ownership of msg->data moves to the queue, and msg->data
is set to NULL to show it. The caller must not free, read or otherwise
use that pointer after the call. Later a receiver takes the message out
with ccol_dynmq_recv_zc(3), ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(3) or
ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc(3), becomes its owner and frees the data. No
bytes are copied at any point; only the pointer and its size move.

On any failure msg is left exactly as the caller gave it, so the caller
still owns msg->data and must free it or send it somewhere else.

A message with msg->data == NULL and msg->size == 0 is valid and
carries no payload. It is the usual way to send a wake-up or an
end-of-stream sentinel through the queue.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq   Dynamic queue to send to
msg  Message to send. On success the ownership of msg->data moves to the
     queue.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            - The function put the message into the queue;
                          msg->data is set to NULL, and the queue owns
                          what it pointed to.
ccol_invalid_args       - dq or msg is NULL, or msg->data is NULL while
                          msg->size is not zero, or msg->data is not
                          NULL while msg->size is 0.
ccol_not_permitted      - A ccol_dynmq_disable_sending() call turned sends
                          off
ccol_container_full     - The queue already holds ccol_max_elem_count
                          messages
ccol_not_enough_memory  - The function could not allocate the list node
                          for the message
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The call never blocks; it returns a success or an error code at once.
A successful send wakes a receiver blocked in ccol_dynmq_recv_zc() or
ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc(), and also one read-direction waiter on the
queue (a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call or a
ccol_event_loop registration). The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_dynmq_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_timed_recv_zc (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_disable_sending (3),
.BR ccol_dynmq_msg_count (3)
